  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 309 AM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nUpper troffing is progressing from the eastern seaboard states and \neastern Canada. The southern stream portion of this will get left \nbehind just off the east coast, remaining just east of NC/VA thru \nmidweek. Also by midweek, another upper low will move southward to \njust off of CA. These are the anchors to an evolving omega \nridge/block over much of the CONUS this week. A storm track remains \nactive over central/southern Canada, but this is a bit too far north \nto seriously bother northern MI.\n\nThe above results in what is generally a pretty quiet forecast, \nespecially for the 2nd half of October. However, in the process of \nejecting our colder airmass today, we'll have a breezy to windy day \nto do it. A handful of Lake MI and Straits area coastal locales are \nalready gusting to around 40mph. The warm advection and associated \nlowering/sharpening inversion will eventually place a limit on \nvertical mixing (especially heading into tonight). But diurnal \nheating/mixing over land increases today. Gusts around 40 mph will \nbe common today in the Straits area and broader Mackinac Co. A few \ngusts around 45 mph (advisory criteria) are probable, but not quite \nenough to warrant a headline, for now.\n\nOtherwise, the strong surface low crossing Hudson Bay and \ncontributing to the tight pressure gradient today will quickly exit \neast. Lots of high pressure surface and aloft after that, at least \nuntil a weak and moisture-starved cold front enters the area \nWednesday. That's replaced by more high pressure until next weekend, \nmaybe. The forecast will have no pops in it, until Sunday of next \nweekend, when a slight chance slips into northern Chippewa Co. \nThat's an extraordinarily quiet forecast.\n\nMax temps jump back above climo today, and stay there for the \nforeseeable future. After today (which is a little cooler), we settle \ninto a regime with highs mainly in the 60s thru the work week. A few \ncooler spots in the eastern UP will be around 60f, and a few warmer \nspots in northern lower MI will take a run at 70f.",
